# Suricata global threshold / suppression file.
# Installed to C:\Program Files\Suricata\threshold.config by 03-configure-suricata.ps1.
#
# Suricata looks for this file whether or not threshold-file is set in suricata.yaml, and
# logs a warning on every start if it is missing. Shipping it both silences that and gives
# you one obvious place to tune noise.
#
# Two mechanisms:
#   suppress  - stop a signature alerting, optionally only for certain hosts
#   threshold - rate-limit a signature (limit / threshold / both)
#
# Syntax reference: https://docs.suricata.io/en/latest/configuration/global-thresholds.html
#
# Note: rules in local.rules use the inline "threshold:" keyword instead, which is a
# separate mechanism. Use this file for signatures you do not own (ET Open, and
# Suricata's own decoder events).


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Checksum decoder events -- NIC offload artifacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are the same false positives that 03-configure-suricata.ps1 addresses properly
# by setting checksum-checks: no and stream.checksum-validation: no. They are suppressed
# here as a belt-and-braces measure, because if the config fix ever regresses these alone
# can generate multiple GB per day and drown every real detection.
#
# SID 2200073 = SURICATA IPv4 invalid checksum
# SID 2200075 = SURICATA TCPv4 invalid checksum
# SID 2200076 = SURICATA UDPv4 invalid checksum
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200073
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200075
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200076


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [fix] Decoder-event flood -- SID 2200002 / 2200121
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Confirmed on a live deployment: SID 2200002 ("SURICATA IPv4 total length
# smaller than header size") fired over a thousand times per minute on a
# normal, healthy endpoint, alongside SID 2200121 ("SURICATA Ethertype
# unknown") showing the same pattern. Unlike the checksum SIDs above, this
# one's root cause was investigated and NOT conclusively identified:
#   - NIC checksum/LSO offloads were confirmed already Disabled (ruled out).
#   - Reproduced with only the active adapter's NPF device (ruled out the
#     second, disconnected adapter as the cause).
#   - packets/drops/invalid-checksum counters were all clean for the run
#     that still showed the flood (ruled out capture-level packet loss).
# Leading theory is a Windows/Npcap capture-path artifact in how frames are
# handed to Suricata's decoder on this NIC, never confirmed with a
# packet-level capture. Suppressed here because, root cause aside, these
# are decoder-level noise (level 3, no source/dest IP or port in the raw
# event) drowning out real signature and visibility alerts at the volume
# observed -- not because the underlying network behavior is understood.
# If you get a packet capture that explains it, this suppression is the
# first thing to revisit.
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200002
suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2200121


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Examples for your own tuning -- all commented out
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Suppress a signature entirely:
# suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2019401

# Suppress only for traffic involving one host (e.g. a backup server that looks odd):
# suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2019401, track by_src, ip 192.168.1.50

# Suppress for a whole subnet:
# suppress gen_id 1, sig_id 2019401, track by_src, ip 192.168.1.0/24

# Rate-limit: at most 1 alert per source per 60s, however many times it matches.
# threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 2019401, type limit, track by_src, count 1, seconds 60

# Only alert once a signature fires 5+ times from one source in 60s (scan-like behaviour).
# threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 2019401, type threshold, track by_src, count 5, seconds 60

# Both: needs 5 hits in 60s to fire, then at most one alert per 60s.
# threshold gen_id 1, sig_id 2019401, type both, track by_src, count 5, seconds 60
